cut back in size in Q3 2026. These are the only issuance data points I found (`pellham-offering`, `utility-credit`).\n- **The capex gap is large.** Pellham plans 2027 capital spending up 18% and Norvane up 12%, mostly on substations and transformers (`utility-capex-2027`). Pellham is the largest single buyer of grid equipment in Caldria.\n- **Pellham is cutting spending now.** After the withdrawal it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes until financing improves. That shows its capex plan depends on equity funding.\n- **Debt is working better than equity.** Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably, even though utility bond spreads widened 30bp in Q3 to 165bp. Funding for 2027 is likely to lean on debt.\n- **Missing data.** The sources have no P/E or price-to-book figures, no sector index performance, and no dilution estimates. I can't quantify valuations or dilution. My conclusion rests on the failed and downsized deals, which are direct evidence of weak demand.\n\n**Implication:** Plans from issuers that can still reach the bond market, like Norvane, look more achievable. Plans that depend on equity, like Pellham's, are at risk of cuts and deferrals. That is a negative for grid-equipment demand from Pellham.","ttft_ms":1296,"type":"result","duration_ms":20922,"uuid":"9f357c06-6ff3-4cc8-9364-629982b65cc6","ttft_stream_ms":801,"time_to_request_ms":58,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":4,"system_prompt":4,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":2,"input_attachments":11,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":2,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":7,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":2,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":0,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":6,"query_setup":1,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":2,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":2,"request_build":5},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":7,"time_to_request_cpu_m